Apply the pending memory update before a state write or a state read
Fold-into: upstream/llama-context

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- A state save after a sequence copy between two KV streams saves old data.

  With kv_unified = false, llama_kv_cache::seq_cp between two streams copies the cell metadata at
  once, but it enqueues the copy of the K and V data, and the next memory update (at the next
  decode) does the copy. A llama_state_seq_get_data of the destination before that decode saves
  the metadata of the source with the old K and V data of the destination. A state load into the
  source of a pending copy is then overwritten by the copy at the next decode. A pending position
  shift (seq_add) has the same effect on a save. The next decode of the sequence then gives wrong
  logits (max diff 0.118 on the tiny model), with no error.

  The change: The four state functions of llama_context (write and read, full and for one
  sequence) run memory_update(false) first. With no pending update, the memory gives
  LLAMA_MEMORY_STATUS_NO_UPDATE and nothing runs. The app uses kv_unified = true and no seq_add,
  thus it has no pending update at a save.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_recurrent--state-pending-stream-copy.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

Files:
    src/llama-context.cpp
--- a/src/llama-context.cpp
+++ b/src/llama-context.cpp
@@ -3481,6 +3481,12 @@ size_t llama_context::state_seq_save_file(llama_seq_id seq_id, const char * file
 size_t llama_context::state_write_data(llama_io_write_i & io) {
     LLAMA_LOG_DEBUG("%s: writing state\n", __func__);
 
+    // The memory applies a sequence copy between two streams (kv_unified = false) and a position
+    // shift at its next update, which runs at the next decode. A state write before that update
+    // saves the old data of the destination, and a state read before it is then overwritten by
+    // the copy. Thus the pending update runs first. With no pending update it does nothing.
+    memory_update(false);
+
     // write model info
     {
         LLAMA_LOG_DEBUG("%s: - writing model info\n", __func__);
@@ -3501,6 +3507,9 @@ size_t llama_context::state_write_data(llama_io_write_i & io) {
 size_t llama_context::state_read_data(llama_io_read_i & io) {
     LLAMA_LOG_DEBUG("%s: reading state\n", __func__);
 
+    // the pending update runs first (refer to state_write_data)
+    memory_update(false);
+
     // read model info
     {
         LLAMA_LOG_DEBUG("%s: - reading model info\n", __func__);
@@ -3525,6 +3534,9 @@ size_t llama_context::state_read_data(llama_io_read_i & io) {
 }
 
 size_t llama_context::state_seq_write_data(llama_io_write_i & io, llama_seq_id seq_id, llama_state_seq_flags flags) {
+    // the pending update runs first (refer to state_write_data)
+    memory_update(false);
+
     if (memory) {
         memory->state_write(io, seq_id, flags);
     }
@@ -3533,6 +3545,9 @@ size_t llama_context::state_seq_write_data(llama_io_write_i & io, llama_seq_id s
 }
 
 size_t llama_context::state_seq_read_data(llama_io_read_i & io, llama_seq_id seq_id, llama_state_seq_flags flags) {
+    // the pending update runs first (refer to state_write_data)
+    memory_update(false);
+
     if (memory) {
         memory->state_read(io, seq_id, flags);
     }
